From: VP Studio
Subject: [PATCH] qemu-cpu: register a woken MTTCG CPU's quantum keeper from the waker

In MTTCG mode a CPU that goes to sleep (WFI) stops its quantum keeper in
wait_for_work(), which detaches its suspending channel from SystemC. When
another CPU wakes it (kick_cb -> set_signaled), the quantum keeper was only
restarted once the woken vCPU thread ran again. If the waker went to sleep
straight away (Linux stop_machine: send an IPI, then WFI), there was a window
with no suspending channel, no timed event and no pending async update, and
SystemC ended the simulation by starvation. The wake-up itself was not lost.

Seen booting Linux on the Ubuntu AArch64 platform with 2 or more CPUs that
start powered off and no keep_alive module: about half of the boots, and up to
10 in 11 under load, end 12 to 17 s after SC_START with no kernel output, at
"alternatives: patching kernel code".

Restart the quantum keeper in set_signaled(), under m_signaled_lock, when it
was stopped for sleeping, and in wait_for_work() stop it under the same lock
only when no wake-up is pending. A wake-up then either comes before the stop
(the quantum keeper keeps running) or after it (the waker restarts it). Kicks
to a running CPU do nothing more than before. Single-threaded TCG and
coroutine mode are unchanged.

diff --git a/qemu-components/common/include/cpu.h b/qemu-components/common/include/cpu.h
index 214249a..49815cd 100644
--- a/qemu-components/common/include/cpu.h
+++ b/qemu-components/common/include/cpu.h
@@ -161,6 +161,11 @@ protected:
     std::atomic<bool> m_signaled;
     std::mutex m_signaled_lock;
     std::condition_variable m_signaled_cond;
+    /*
+     * MTTCG: the quantum keeper was stopped because this CPU went to sleep in
+     * wait_for_work(). Protected by m_signaled_lock.
+     */
+    bool m_qk_stopped_for_sleep = false;
 
     std::shared_ptr<gs::tlm_quantumkeeper_extended> m_qk;
     std::atomic<bool> m_finished = false;
@@ -278,6 +283,19 @@ protected:
         assert(!m_coroutines);
         if (m_inst.get_tcg_mode() != QemuInstance::TCG_SINGLE) {
             std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m_signaled_lock);
+            /*
+             * Restart the quantum keeper of a sleeping CPU here, in the thread
+             * that wakes it, rather than when the woken CPU thread gets to run
+             * again. Otherwise, if the waker goes to sleep at once (Linux
+             * stop_machine: send an IPI, then WFI), there is a window in which
+             * no CPU has a suspending channel attached and nothing else keeps
+             * SystemC busy, and the simulation ends by starvation although a
+             * CPU was just woken up.
+             */
+            if (m_qk_stopped_for_sleep && !m_finished) {
+                m_qk->start();
+                m_qk_stopped_for_sleep = false;
+            }
             m_signaled = true;
             m_signaled_cond.notify_all();
         } else {
@@ -349,16 +367,30 @@ protected:
     void wait_for_work()
     {
         SCP_TRACE(())("Wait for work");
-        m_qk->stop();
+        const bool mttcg = !m_coroutines && m_inst.get_tcg_mode() != QemuInstance::TCG_SINGLE;
+        /* In MTTCG mode the QK is stopped below, under m_signaled_lock. */
+        if (!mttcg || m_finished) m_qk->stop();
         if (m_finished) return;
 
         if (m_coroutines) {
             m_on_sysc.run_on_sysc([this]() { wait(m_external_ev); });
         } else {
-            if (m_inst.get_tcg_mode() != QemuInstance::TCG_SINGLE) {
+            if (mttcg) {
                 std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m_signaled_lock);
+                /*
+                 * Stop the QK only if no wake-up is pending, and remember why,
+                 * so that set_signaled() restarts it from the waking thread.
+                 * Both run under m_signaled_lock, so a wake-up either comes
+                 * before (the QK keeps running) or after the stop (the waker
+                 * restarts it); the CPU is never awake without its QK.
+                 */
+                if (!m_signaled) {
+                    m_qk->stop();
+                    m_qk_stopped_for_sleep = true;
+                }
                 m_signaled_cond.wait(lock, [this] { return m_signaled || m_finished; });
                 m_signaled = false;
+                m_qk_stopped_for_sleep = false;
             } else {
                 std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m_inst.g_signaled_lock);
                 m_inst.g_signaled_cond.wait(lock, [this] { return m_inst.g_signaled || m_finished; });
